OPEN DECISIONS — resolve before publishing, then delete this section

These are the same decisions as in the SDK's licence, plus items 9 and 10 which
are specific to the map. Decide them once and apply to both files.

1. LEGAL ENTITY. "Proximi.io" is used throughout as the licensor. Replace it
   with the registered company name, company number and registered address of
   the entity that actually owns the software and contracts with customers. A
   licence granted by a brand name rather than a legal person is weak.

2. GOVERNING LAW AND FORUM (clause 12). Not decided, and deliberately not
   guessed. Must match the SDK's licence.

3. LIABILITY CAP (clause 10.2). "Fees paid in the preceding twelve months" is a
   placeholder figure, not a decision anyone has made. Must match the SDK's,
   and must be compatible with the cap in the standard commercial agreement,
   since clause 7.2 makes that one prevail.

4. WARRANTY POSTURE (clause 9). Drafted as a full "as is" disclaimer. Clause
   9.3's safety-critical exclusion is worth a careful read for the map in
   particular, because customers use it for wayfinding in public venues, and
   "emergency egress" is an obvious thing for a venue operator to want.

5. THIRD-PARTY NOTICES (clause 8). This side is in reasonable shape: the
   release pipeline already copies `NOTICES.md` and
   `ThirdParty-MapLibre-LICENSE.txt` into this repository beside the licence.
   The SDK's side is not — see docs/licensing-notes.md in the SDK repository.
   Confirm the two products should stay asymmetric or should be made to match.

6. TERMINATION AND SHIPPED APPS (clause 11.3). The draft says end users keep
   working copies after termination. Confirm that customer-friendly reading is
   intended, and that it does not conflict with the token shutoff the
   commercial agreement presumably allows.

7. CLAUSE 3.4 (COMPETING USE). Drafted broadly. Broad non-compete language in a
   licence can be unenforceable in some jurisdictions and can scare off
   customers who build adjacent products. Decide how wide it should really be.

8. WHETHER ONE LICENCE FILE SHOULD COVER BOTH PRODUCTS. Right now there are two
   near-identical files that must be kept in sync by hand, which they will not
   be. An alternative is one Proximi.io Mobile SDK licence, referenced by both
   distributions, with a per-product third-party annex. That is a commercial
   decision, not a drafting one.

9. MAP DATA AND TILE TERMS (clause 8.3). The draft says map data terms are
   between the customer and their tile provider, and that Proximi.io does not
   license OpenStreetMap data. Confirm that is true of every delivery model —
   if Proximi.io ever serves or proxies tiles, or ships offline tile packages
   with venue data, that sentence is wrong and the obligation shifts.

10. MAPLIBRE VERSION IN THE TEXT (clause 8.1). The version is named as "6.29.0
    at the time this draft was written" and otherwise points at
    `Package.swift`. The licence file is copied verbatim by the release script
    and is NOT templated, so a hard version number here will go stale silently.
    Either keep the soft phrasing, or decide to template it — which would need
    a change to scripts/publish-binary-release.sh that was deliberately not
    made here.
